<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kokand</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kokand is a city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Fergana Valley</a> of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> and was the capital of the Khanate of Kokand from the eighteenth century. The khanate was one of the Central Asian states conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> in the nineteenth century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> annexed Kokand in 1876 as part of its expansion into Turkestan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kokand.jpg" alt="Kokand" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Екатерина Борисова · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9A%D0%BE%D0%BA%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B4._%D0%A0%D1%83%D1%81%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE-%D0%90%D0%B7%D0%B8%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%B1%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%BA_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1763 he occupied the lands between Tashkent and <b>Kokand</b> at the plea of Fazil Biy of Kokand and Kyrgyz chieftains against Qing expansion, withdrawing by 1764 when no alliance formed, and sent an embassy to Beijing with a letter, probably a fath-nama celebrating Panipat, and gifts of…</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The subjugation of the Central Asian khanates of Bukhara (1868), Khiva (1873) and <b>Kokand</b> (1876) brought the frontier to the borders of Afghanistan and British India, and generated the 'Great Game' rivalry with Britain that governed Central Asian political geography until the Anglo-Russian Convention of 1907.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Uzbek tribes from north of the Aral Sea invaded in 1501, and before the Russians arrived the country was divided between the Emirate of Bukhara and the khanates of Khiva and <b>Kokand</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They exploited the emirate's conflicts with Russia and <b>Kokand</b>, at times accepting nominal Bukharan overlordship.</blockquote><a href="/state/beylik-of-shahrisabz">Beylik of Shahrisabz</a></li><li><blockquote>His successors built <b>Kokand</b> as their capital, and Narbuta Bi strengthened the state in the late 18th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/khanate-of-kokand">Khanate of Kokand</a></li><li><blockquote>In November 1917 the Fourth Extraordinary Regional Muslim Congress met in <b>Kokand</b> and proclaimed the Turkestan Autonomy within a future democratic Russian federation.</blockquote><a href="/state/kokand-autonomy">Kokand Autonomy</a></li></ul></section>
